Part D: second-order lifting test at near-tight real designs (LP-optimal rho)
  Lifting a real double zero at x_j into a pair x_j +- i v changes Sigma by 2 (2 pi v)^2 C_j + O(v^4),
  C_j = sum_k m_k q(x_j - x_k),  q(u) = int rho(a) a^2 cos(2 pi a u) da.  A violation of (**) from a tight
  design needs C_j < 0.  Designs: n simple zeros + one double zero, positions optimised for the real ratio.
  q(0) = 0.148755 ; min_u q(u) = -0.086492
  n= 6: real-design ratio 1.004578 ; C_j = +0.250076 ; lifted ratios v=.05,.1,.2: [1.009584 1.025455 1.103185] ; joint re-optimisation -> ratio 1.004578 at v = 2.1e-09
  n=10: real-design ratio 1.003384 ; C_j = +0.249175 ; lifted ratios v=.05,.1,.2: [1.006947 1.018244 1.073593] ; joint re-optimisation -> ratio 1.003384 at v = 1.9e-09

Status: Lemma 1 (naive transfer refuted by a lone pair) PROVED in memo and CHECKED above;
        (**) for complex Z with the CG-optimal rho: CONJECTURE supported numerically, not proved;
        no unconditional bound above the Montgomery-Taylor value is certified by this script.
